The main idea behind our proposed loss function is the avoidance of the over- or under-sampling problems mentioned above with the assumption that the distances (or similarities) between descriptors of the same class (i.e., matching pairs) or different classes (i.e., non-matching pairs) are samples from two distinct distributions. This allows us to formulate a loss function (for the embedding case) that globally tries to: 1) minimise the variance of the two distributions and the mean value of the distances between matching pairs, and 2) maximise the mean value of the distances between non-matching pairs.  Fig.~\ref{fig:global_loss}-(a) depicts the reasoning behind the design of the proposed global loss function, which is defined for the feature embedding case by:
